course: dictionary
lesson: 13658
title: Inflict: to make someone suffer
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To inflict something means to make someone or something suffer it. The thing inflicted is usually bad: pain, harm, damage or punishment. «The storm inflicted serious damage on the village.» The storm caused the damage, and the village had to suffer it.
explain: Inflict is a formal word. You will often find it in news reports, history books and safety warnings. «The army inflicted a heavy defeat on the enemy.» Ask yourself who did the harm, and who had to suffer it. The word tells you who caused the harm.
explain: You can also use inflict in a joking way. «My little brother inflicted his terrible singing on the whole car.» Nobody was really hurt. The family just had to put up with something unwanted. When you meet inflict, check: did someone have to suffer something bad? If yes, inflict fits.
fact: Inflict is a verb: inflict, inflicts, inflicted, inflicting.
fact: To inflict something means to make someone or something suffer it, such as pain, harm, damage or punishment.
fact: Common phrases are «inflict damage on», «inflict pain on» and «inflict a defeat».
fact: «Self-inflicted» means you caused the harm to yourself.
fact: In a joking sense, «inflict» means to make someone put up with something unwanted, like bad singing.
exercise: «The earthquake inflicted terrible damage on the old bridge.» What does inflicted mean here? | Mended and painted | Watched carefully | *Caused, so the bridge had to suffer | Opened to traffic
exercise: The team wanted to inflict a defeat on their rivals. Which word means the same as inflict? | Mend | *Cause | Forget | Guess
exercise: «The storm inflicted damage on the town.» Who did the inflicting? | Everybody in the town | The damage | *The storm | The town
test: «The bad weather inflicted heavy losses on the farmers.» What does inflicted mean? | Painted their barns | Gave them a prize | *Caused them to suffer | Hid from them
test: Which of these is a self-inflicted problem? | Missing the bus | Being stung by a bee | Getting a cold | *Hurting your own foot by dropping a heavy box on it
exam: «My brother inflicted his terrible singing on us all the way to the beach.» What does inflicted mean here? | Made us laugh until we cried | *Made us put up with something unwanted | Gave us a present | Taught us a new song
